      "question_id": "q-20260830-nyadran",
      "date": "2026-08-30",
      "kind": "guess",
      "topic_id": "top_01KZXQ6BREK9FPFXH2K5JANWA1",
      "topic_slug": "ghosts-ancestors-and-remembrance",
      "asker_locale": null,
      "target_locale": null,
      "text": "Guess: which community tends the ancestral graves just before Ramadan begins — cleaning them, praying together, and closing with the whole village sitting down to one shared meal?",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "id",
          "emoji": "🇮🇩",
          "label": "Indonesia"
        },
        {
          "id": "hi",
          "emoji": "🇮🇳",
          "label": "India"
        },
        {
          "id": "zh-TW",
          "emoji": "🇹🇼",
          "label": "Taiwan"
        },
        {
          "id": "ja",
          "emoji": "🇯🇵",
          "label": "Japan"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "id",
      "explain": "Indonesia, in Java. It is Nyadran, done in the month of Ruwah before Ramadan begins. The word comes from the Sanskrit sraddha, faith. The sequence is fixed — clean the graves, then pray together, then close with kembul bujono, everyone eating one meal side by side. It began in pre-Islamic belief and later merged with Javanese Islamic practice. Like Qingming it is grave-tending, but it aligns not with a particular date so much as with the fact that fasting starts next month."
    },
