      "question_id": "q-20260901-moving-childrens-day",
      "date": "2026-09-01",
      "kind": "guess",
      "topic_id": "top_01KZXQ6BR1T8YKVJ0RQ7NZG4MV",
      "topic_slug": "childrens-day",
      "asker_locale": null,
      "target_locale": null,
      "text": "Guess: in which community does Children's Day not even have a fixed date — re-proclaimed each year by the head of state, and moved at least four times in forty years?",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "en",
          "emoji": "🇺🇸",
          "label": "the United States"
        },
        {
          "id": "id",
          "emoji": "🇮🇩",
          "label": "Indonesia"
        },
        {
          "id": "pt-BR",
          "emoji": "🇧🇷",
          "label": "Brazil"
        },
        {
          "id": "zh-CN",
          "emoji": "🇨🇳",
          "label": "China"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "en",
      "explain": "The United States. It is not a statutory holiday; it exists as a presidential proclamation reissued each year: Reagan put it on 8 August in 1982, Clinton on 11 October in 1998, George W. Bush on 9 June in 2002, Obama moved it to 20 November from 2013, and Biden kept 20 November in 2024. A proclamation is not a law, brings no day off, and the next occupant can move it. By contrast Indonesia's National Children's Day was fixed on 23 July by Presidential Decision No. 44 of 1984, and Brazil's 12 October coincides with Our Lady of Aparecida, already a national holiday."
