      "topic_slug": "harvest-and-gratitude",
      "asker_locale": null,
      "target_locale": null,
      "text": "Guess: in which community does Farmers' Day fall not after the harvest but on the first day of spring — that is, before the work has even begun?",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "zh-TW",
          "emoji": "🇹🇼",
          "label": "Taiwan"
        },
        {
          "id": "ja",
          "emoji": "🇯🇵",
          "label": "Japan"
        },
        {
          "id": "zh-CN",
          "emoji": "🇨🇳",
          "label": "China"
        },
        {
          "id": "id",
          "emoji": "🇮🇩",
          "label": "Indonesia"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "zh-TW",
      "explain": "Taiwan. The Act on Commemorative Days and Holidays says plainly: \"Farmers' Day: fixed on the day of Lichun.\" That is the reverse of harvest days elsewhere — Thanksgiving, China's farmers' harvest festival and Japan's Labour Thanksgiving all sit after the crop is in, while this one sits before the work starts: what it marks is not what has been gathered but the fact of going back to the field. The date is set by astronomy, so whether it lands on 3, 4 or 5 February must be looked up each year in the Central Weather Administration's calendar tables. It is not a day off."
