      "question_id": "q-20260904-valentine-circulars",
      "date": "2026-09-04",
      "kind": "guess",
      "topic_id": "top_01KZRR9MEXM6D9S4DF4DDXJNES",
      "topic_slug": "affection-and-reciprocity",
      "asker_locale": null,
      "target_locale": null,
      "text": "Guess: in which community does the official paperwork mention Valentine's Day in order to tell students not to mark it?",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "id",
          "emoji": "🇮🇩",
          "label": "Indonesia"
        },
        {
          "id": "ja",
          "emoji": "🇯🇵",
          "label": "Japan"
        },
        {
          "id": "zh-CN",
          "emoji": "🇨🇳",
          "label": "China"
        },
        {
          "id": "pt-BR",
          "emoji": "🇧🇷",
          "label": "Brazil"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "id",
      "explain": "Indonesia. The fourteenth of February is not a holiday, and the institutional trace it leaves runs the other way: around the date each year, education offices in a number of local governments issue circulars — surat edaran — asking schools in their area to discourage students from marking it. Depok in West Java issued one in 2023, Pasuruan Regency in East Java in 2017, and the Riau Islands provincial education office advised its senior and vocational secondary schools likewise. The stated reason is plain: it does not fit religious, social and cultural norms. By contrast, what a state may recognise is the other one: China entered the Qixi festival on the first national list of intangible cultural heritage in 2006, while 14 February has no institutional standing there either."
